<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Underground Heating Oil Tanks &amp; PLIA $75,000 Grants</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Navigating buried oil tanks in King and Snohomish counties: how to conduct a tank sweep, understand decommissioning records, protect your contract disclosures, and access Washington State grant funding.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Why Buried Tanks Shouldn't Derail Your Purchase</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Finding out a home has an underground heating oil tank can sound intimidating, but it shouldn't derail your transaction. Buried fuel tanks are extremely common across King and Snohomish counties in homes built before 1985. With a simple tank sweep, clean decommissioning records, and Washington's PLIA grant program, managing a tank is straightforward once you know the playbook.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Underground Fuel Tanks 101: Why They Exist in the PNW</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Before natural gas pipelines expanded across Seattle, Edmonds, Shoreline, and Lynnwood in the mid-to-late 20th century, heating oil was the standard residential heating fuel across Washington State. Most single-family homes buried a 300 to 1,000-gallon steel fuel tank directly in the yard.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Over time, as homes converted to natural gas or modern heat pumps, many of these tanks were disconnected and left underground. Because bare steel rusts when exposed to moist Pacific Northwest soil, an unmonitored buried tank can eventually corrode and leak petroleum into topsoil or groundwater.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Decommissioning vs. Full Removal</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      When dealing with an underground heating oil tank in King or Snohomish County, sellers and buyers generally encounter two distinct pathways.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">In-Place Mitigation</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Tank Decommissioning</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Process &amp; Scope</span>
          <p class="text-body">Pumping out leftover oil, triple-rinsing the tank interior, and filling the void with inert expanding foam or slurry.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Permits &amp; Soil Testing</span>
          <p class="text-body">Requires a local Fire Department permit and official contractor certificate. Soil samples are drawn through bottom borings during cleaning.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Lender Acceptance</span>
          <p class="text-body">Widely accepted by major lenders and buyers if backed by an official municipal Fire Department Decommissioning Certificate.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Total Elimination</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Full Tank Excavation</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Process &amp; Scope</span>
          <p class="text-body">Excavating the yard with heavy machinery, digging out the steel tank completely, and hauling it away for scrap disposal.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Permits &amp; Soil Testing</span>
          <p class="text-body">Requires local mechanical/building permits and environmental soil disposal receipts. Direct visual soil inspection and lab testing from the open pit.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Lender Acceptance</span>
          <p class="text-body">The gold standard for total peace of mind. Completely eliminates any future buried liability or subsequent buyer hesitation.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Washington State PLIA $75,000 Grant Program (HOLG)</h2>
    <div class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
        One of Washington State's best financial safety nets for homeowners is the <strong>Heating Oil Loan &amp; Grant (HOLG) Program</strong>, administered by the Pollution Liability Insurance Agency (PLIA).
      </p>

      <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Grant Funding Up to $75,000</span>
          <p class="text-body">Income-qualified homeowners who register their heating oil tank with PLIA can receive up to $75,000 in grant funds to cover tank removal and soil remediation costs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">No Out-of-Pocket Crisis</span>
          <p class="text-body">If an old tank leak is discovered during a soil test, PLIA grants can directly reimburse certified environmental contractors to clean up the soil.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="calc-field-group">
        <span class="calc-field-label">How to Qualify</span>
        <p class="text-body">The program covers residential properties with active or abandoned heating oil tanks used for space heating. Registration must be filed directly with PLIA before major cleanup begins.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Property Scenario Matrix</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">High Priority</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Active Oil Heat System</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Active or un-decommissioned tanks qualify for Washington State PLIA grants up to $75,000 for decommissioning or soil cleanup. Register with PLIA immediately before starting any conversion work.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Low Risk</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Certified Decommissioned</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          You have official municipal Fire Department documentation on file. Keep a copy ready for Form 17 disclosures (sellers) or lender underwriting review (buyers) to clear escrow contingencies.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Testing Required</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Unverified / Missing Records</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          A tank without fire department certificates should be verified with a quick soil boring test ($300-$500) or ground-penetrating radar sweep to satisfy buyer and lender requirements.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Buyer vs. Seller Protocol</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">For Homebuyers</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Due Diligence Protocol</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Order a Tank Sweep ($150–$200)</span>
          <p class="text-body">During your inspection window, hire a specialist to scan the yard with ground-penetrating radar to confirm whether an undisclosed buried tank exists.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Verify Decommissioning Certificates</span>
          <p class="text-body">Insist on seeing official Fire Department Decommissioning Certificates and soil sample lab reports before waiving environmental contingencies.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">For Sellers</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Listing Preparation Strategy</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Locate Historic Paperwork Early</span>
          <p class="text-body">Dig up your fire department decommissioning permits, PLIA registration forms, and contractor receipts before listing on the NWMLS.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Disclose Accurately</span>
          <p class="text-body">Complete Section 7 (Environmental) on Form 17 accurately to protect yourself from post-closing buyer liability and potential legal disputes.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

